From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: place a node that reports no uplink of its own

A node is placed in the backhaul tree only by what it says about its own
uplink: the Backhaul STA MLD Config TLV (0xE1, sent only by our map-agent)
or a Link Metric Response. A MediaTek R3 agent sends neither, and a wired
agent has no bBSS for the Link Metric fallback to match, so both stayed at
depth -1 for good, with no edge in topology_link and nothing in the picture.

The tree now takes the uplink from three sources, in this order:

1. the child's own claim, as before (source "child");
2. the parent listing the child's backhaul STA among its associated
   clients - by the bSTA the child named in its Backhaul STA Capability
   Report, or by its bSTA MLD and link addresses (source "parent-assoc");
3. 1905 neighbours in either direction, for a node none of the above
   covers (source "neighbor", or "neighbor-mutual" when each side
   lists the other).

A 1905 neighbour is not a physical neighbour: Topology Discovery goes to
01:80:c2:00:00:13, which a Linux bridge forwards. Seen on hardware
2026-09-18: the MediaTek box wired to x8 was listed by our corridor agent,
associated to x8 over Wi-Fi, as a neighbour on its bSTA, next to x8. So
Y is not taken as X's parent when Y hears X on the interface it hears its
own parent on, and of the remaining candidates the deepest wins. A device
is placed from neighbours one per round, after the reports settle, so that
what the reports say always takes precedence.

For that the neighbour data has to survive: the tree build flushed every
device, including the interfaces and neighbours handle_topology_response()
had just stored. It now keeps the devices of known agents and resets only
their backhaul info.

topology_link gets a "source" column (schema v6), the media type of the
uplink where known, and the parent's own address instead of the
controller's when the parent interface is unknown. dump_topology shows the
source.

Also in the AP Operational BSS TLV: an SSID declared longer than 32 bytes
discarded the whole Topology Response. The declared length now moves the
offset, the SSID is what comes before the first zero, and a TLV that ends
early stops the parse without sweeping radios it did not get to read.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c b/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c
index fd0020b..f8400d9 100644
--- a/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c
@@ -4246,6 +4246,8 @@ int COMMAND(dump_topology)(void *priv, void *args, void *out)
 		}
 		blobmsg_add_u32(bb, "depth", dev->bh.depth);
 		blobmsg_add_u32(bb, "depth_wifi", dev->bh.wifi_hops_from_root);
+		if (dev->bh.parent)
+			blobmsg_add_string(bb, "source", bh_source_str(dev->bh.source));
 
 		blobmsg_close_table(bb, tt);
 	}
diff --git a/src/cntlr_map.c b/src/cntlr_map.c
index e4fb280..5b9a745 100644
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -696,12 +696,14 @@ int handle_topology_response(void *cntlr, struct cmdu_buff *cmdu, struct node *n
 		struct tlv_ap_oper_bss *tlv;
 		uint8_t *tv_data;
 		int i, offset = 0;
+		int oper_len;
 
 		tlv = (struct tlv_ap_oper_bss *)tv[TOPOLOGY_RESPONSE_AP_OPERATIONAL_BSS_IDX][0]->data;
 		if (!tlv)
 			return -1;
 
 		tv_data = (uint8_t *)tlv;
+		oper_len = tlv_length(tv[TOPOLOGY_RESPONSE_AP_OPERATIONAL_BSS_IDX][0]);
 
 		offset += 1; /* num_radio */
 
@@ -719,6 +721,9 @@ int handle_topology_response(void *cntlr, struct cmdu_buff *cmdu, struct node *n
 			struct netif_iface *p = NULL;
 			int j;
 
+			if (offset + 6 + 1 > oper_len)
+				goto oper_bss_end;
+
 			r = cntlr_node_add_radio(c, n, &tv_data[offset]);
 			if (!r)
 				return -1; /* FIXME: continue + proper offset shift */
@@ -741,22 +746,34 @@ int handle_topology_response(void *cntlr, struct cmdu_buff *cmdu, struct node *n
 				struct netif_iface *fh;
 				uint8_t ssidlen = 0;
 
+				if (offset + 6 + 1 > oper_len)
+					goto oper_bss_end;
 				offset += 6; /* macaddr */
 				ssidlen = tv_data[offset];
-				if (ssidlen > 32) {
-					warn("%s: Discard Topology Response. Invalid ssidlen %d \
-					     in AP Operational BSS TLV\n", __func__, ssidlen);
-					return -1;
-				}
 				offset += 1; /* ssidlen */
 
+				/* Same trap as in the BSS Configuration Report: a
+				 * MediaTek R3 agent declares the SSID field longer
+				 * than 32 and pads it with zeros. Discarding the
+				 * whole Topology Response for that lost every BSS of
+				 * the node. The declared length is what moves the
+				 * offset; the SSID is what comes before the first
+				 * zero, at most 32 bytes.
+				 */
+				if (offset + ssidlen > oper_len) {
+					warn("%s: AP Operational BSS TLV ends inside an SSID\n",
+					     __func__);
+					goto oper_bss_end;
+				}
+
 				fh = cntlr_radio_add_iface(c, r, macaddr);
 				if (!fh)
 					return -1; /* FIXME: continue + proper offset shift */
 
 				memset(fh->bss->ssid, 0, sizeof(fh->bss->ssid));
-				memcpy(fh->bss->ssid, &tv_data[offset], ssidlen);
-				fh->bss->ssidlen = ssidlen;
+				memcpy(fh->bss->ssid, &tv_data[offset],
+				       ssidlen > 32 ? 32 : ssidlen);
+				fh->bss->ssidlen = strlen(fh->bss->ssid);
 				offset += ssidlen; /* ssid */
 
 				/* Update measurement time */
@@ -766,6 +783,11 @@ int handle_topology_response(void *cntlr, struct cmdu_buff *cmdu, struct node *n
 	
 		cntlr_node_sweep_radios(c, n);	/* reconcile stale radios (fix b) */
 		cntlr_node_sweep_ifaces(c, n, pass_start);	/* 989: stale ifaces */
+
+		/* A TLV that ends early leaves the sweep out: what it did not
+		 * list is not gone, we just did not get to read it. */
+oper_bss_end:
+		;
 	}
 
 	/* map profile info stored for each node */
